The Human-English works in a complete lattice, with bottom the supremum of the empty set and top the supremum of the whole lattice, and defines \(x \ll y\) to mean that \(y\) lies in the interior, for the induced topology, of the principal upset of \(x\). Scott gives the relation no name. `WayBelow` is that relation, witnessed by a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\) contained in the principal upset of \(x\), and the infix \(\ll\) is notation for it. `scottOpen_wayBelow` proves that \(\{z \mid x \ll z\}\) is Scott-open, which is not one of the seven clauses of [Proposition 2.2](1972_Proposition_2_2.md): a witnessing neighbourhood of the supremum of a directed set meets that set, and the same neighbourhood witnesses the relation at the meeting point. `directedOn_wayBelow` proves that \(\{x \mid x \ll y\}\) is directed in any complete lattice, because the join of two such elements is again way below \(y\) by Proposition 2.2(ii). `sInf_wayBelow` proves that the infimum of a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\) is way below \(y\), because that neighbourhood contains \(y\) and is contained in the principal upset of its infimum. **Theorem:** yes — the interior definition is formalized. **Proof:** partial — the definition is a definition, and the openness, directedness, and neighbourhood-infimum facts are proved.
